/*
  Here we show that a message channel, which can form the basis of, in effect,
  references to mutable object-like processes (in the style of Kahn and
  Saraswat), can be implemented on top of the primitive concepts provided by
  Llull-02.
  Note that the channel preserves the order of the messages it carries.

  No way is proposed (as yet) to close off a channel so as to indicate that there
  will be no more messages. But a reason to do that might conceivably arise
  as we explore applications.

  The strategy of this implementation involves our use of a record pattern
  {
    car,
    cdr
  }.

  An asker to such a record will represent the authority to receive a message.
  When there is no message available yet, the asker will remain unresolved.
  Once it is resolved, it will carry an instance of the structure. The
  `car` field will provide the message, and the `cdr` field the next
  representation of the channel, for receiving the next message, if any.

  The sending authority will be represented by the teller corresponding to the
  above-mentioned asker.

  There is no need to export any programming artifacts hence, as client code
  can directly implement the operations, using the above structure as a guide.

  Example of sending a message:

    _next_account := ! next_account .
    _my_account := {
      car : {
        verb : # deposit ,
        params_args : [
          amount : $20.59 ,
          _response : ! what_happen ,
        ] ,
      } ,
      cdr : next_account
    } .
*/
